Define storage as preservation of a usable material state (@cs-storage-envelope: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @chemical-supply-systems, #source "../sources/Chemical-Warehousing-HSE-HSG71.lingua#r_6MKP347GP6BY1H1PCKCJKAV91R", #source "../sources/Flammable-Liquid-Tanks-HSE-HSG176.lingua#r_3J96NXWJF945DTPAPBRQPT177R", #source "../sources/Good-Storage-and-Distribution-WHO.lingua#r_64EPQ96J7NE7QCWQ28SPXJ8V4D", #chemical-supply-systems "../../../../stem/chemistry/industrial-chemistry/operation/Equipment-materials-surfaces-corrosion-and-process-state.lingua#r_6FT29RK2NR7R7D4ZPNJKEEASGR") { r_1FWR6G3QPYFVYX7RPM2FXZEHKJ
**Read:** [Chemical warehousing — HSE HSG71](../sources/Chemical-Warehousing-HSE-HSG71.lingua#r_6MKP347GP6BY1H1PCKCJKAV91R), the complete guide for packaged chemical storage; [Storage of flammable liquids in tanks — HSE HSG176](../sources/Flammable-Liquid-Tanks-HSE-HSG176.lingua#r_3J96NXWJF945DTPAPBRQPT177R), the complete applicable tank-storage guide; [Good storage and distribution practices for medical products — WHO](../sources/Good-Storage-and-Distribution-WHO.lingua#r_64EPQ96J7NE7QCWQ28SPXJ8V4D), the whole quality/storage and rotation discussion.

After the shared storage/transfer Record, define the storage envelope for the chosen material: composition, temperature/pressure, atmosphere, contact materials, moisture/light exposure where relevant, time and evidence. A nominal amount in a container is not the same as a quantity still fit for use. Storage also requires access, segregation, inspection, utilities and a way to remove or return material.

Read the complete relevant storage guide and product manual, then draw the actual material path through receipt, holding, issue and residual handling. State what must be measured or observed to determine whether the envelope was maintained. A single current temperature may not establish the previous temperature history; an intact label may not establish package integrity.

Compare a short inventory policy with a larger continuity reserve. More reserve can reduce exposure to delivery delays while increasing age, occupied capacity, inspection and eventual disposal. Model these consequences on the same usable-material basis. Investigate a delayed lot, a damaged package and a loss of required utility. Explain what information would justify continued use, re-evaluation or rejection, without inventing a universal shelf life from a general handbook example.

